  "worked_solution": "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & k\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =1\\cdot k-(-1)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =k+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $k+3\\ne 0$ if $k\\ne -3.$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the third part of the invertible matrix theorem, the unique solution to  $Ax=0$ is the trivial one (zero).\n\n- Statement III is true by the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nFinally, only statements I and III are true.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
